\section{Statistical tests}{
\subsection{Numeric variables: omnibus test}{

Does the variable differ between any of the groups?
\itemize{
\item \code{method = "parametric"} (default): one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA,
F-test of the linear model \code{variable ~ group}; weighted least squares if
\code{weight} is given). Effect size: eta squared
(\code{\link[effectsize:eta_squared]{effectsize::eta_squared()}}), the share of the variance explained by
the groups, with confidence interval.
\item \code{method = "nonparametric"}: Kruskal-Wallis rank sum test
(\code{\link[stats:kruskal.test]{stats::kruskal.test()}}), which compares the distributions of the ranks
instead of the means and is robust to outliers and skewed or ordinal
data. Effect size: rank epsilon squared
(\code{\link[effectsize:rank_epsilon_squared]{effectsize::rank_epsilon_squared()}}). Weights are ignored.
}
}

\subsection{Numeric variables: comparisons}{

Which groups differ from the reference? All comparisons are \emph{linear
contrasts} of the group means of one linear model \code{variable ~ group},
estimated with \code{\link[modelbased:estimate_contrasts]{modelbased::estimate_contrasts()}}:
\itemize{
\item The standard errors are heteroscedasticity-consistent (\code{vcov = "HC3"}),
so the groups may have different variances (as in Welch's t-test).
\item Each contrast is tested with a t-test (degrees of freedom: residual
degrees of freedom of the model).
\item If \code{weight} is given, the model is fitted with weighted least squares,
so the group means and the total are weighted.
\item The p-values are adjusted for the number of comparisons within each
variable (\code{p_adjust}, default Holm), reported as \code{p_adj}.
}

The reference (\code{reference}) defines the contrasts:
\itemize{
\item \code{"total"} (default): each group vs. the \strong{total including the group},
i.e. the (weighted) mean of all respondents, as shown by the "Total" bar
of \code{\link[=eda_plot]{eda_plot()}}. The contrast is \eqn{\bar{y}_g - \sum_k p_k \bar{y}_k},
with \eqn{p_k} the (weighted) share of group \eqn{k}. Its standard error
accounts for the group being part of the total; a simple two-sample test
of the group against the total would wrongly treat them as independent
samples.
\item \code{"rest"}: each group vs. the \strong{rest of the sample} (all respondents not
in the group). As \eqn{\bar{y}_g - \bar{y}_{total} = (1 - p_g)(\bar{y}_g
  - \bar{y}_{rest})}, this test has exactly the same t- and p-values as
\code{"total"}; only the reported difference is larger.
\item \code{"meandev"}: each group vs. the \strong{unweighted mean of the group means}
(deviation or "effect" coding, \code{comparison = "meandev"} in
\code{\link[modelbased:estimate_contrasts]{modelbased::estimate_contrasts()}}). Every group counts equally,
regardless of its size. This equals \code{"total"} only if all groups are of
the same size.
\item \code{"groups"}: \strong{all pairs of groups} (\code{comparison = "pairwise"}).
}

With \code{method = "nonparametric"}, the same contrasts are computed on the
ranks of the variable (rank-transformation approach of Conover & Iman,
1981), i.e. the groups are compared by their mean ranks; weights are
ignored. The estimates are then reported as medians, and the standard
errors and confidence intervals (on the rank scale) are omitted.

\code{direction} tells whether the group (\code{group1}) is \code{"higher"} or \code{"lower"}
than the reference (by the contrast of the means or of the mean ranks).
}

\subsection{Categorical variables}{

Pearson's chi-square test of independence of the variable and the groups
(\code{\link[stats:chisq.test]{stats::chisq.test()}}, without continuity correction). Effect size:
Cramér's V (\code{\link[effectsize:cramers_v]{effectsize::cramers_v()}}, not bias-corrected). If weighted,
the weighted contingency table is rescaled to the unweighted sample size
before testing, so that the weights do not inflate the sample size. There
are no comparisons of single groups for categorical variables.
}

\subsection{Limitations}{

The weighted tests treat the weights as precision weights of a linear
model. This is an approximation; for design-based inference (strata,
clusters, calibration weights), use the \code{survey} package. Many tests (many
variables and groups) increase the chance of false positives; the
p-values are only adjusted within each variable, not across variables.

For a detailed plot of a single variable with further tests (e.g. robust
or Bayesian), see \code{\link[=eda_plot_detail]{eda_plot_detail()}}.
}
